    "sections": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Frogs (1972) kicks off the eco-horror trend of the 1970s with delicious B-movie flair. Ray Milland stars as a wheelchair-bound patriarch whose family estate becomes ground zero for an amphibian uprising. While <a href=\"/movie/38761\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"38761\">Night of the Lepus</a> (1972) gave us killer rabbits and <a href=\"/movie/42218\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"42218\">Empire of the Ants</a> (1977) featured giant insects, Frogs stands out for using mostly real animals filmed in menacing ways rather than relying on creature effects. The film taps into growing environmental consciousness following the first Earth Day in 1970, turning nature's revenge into exploitation entertainment."
      },
