      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The eco-horror wave gained momentum through the 70s with increasingly outlandish premises. Food of the Gods (1976) adapted H.G. Wells into a story of giant rats and wasps, while <a href=\"/movie/86239\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"86239\">The Pack</a> (1977) unleashed killer dogs on a vacation island. These films reflected growing anxiety about pollution, chemical testing, and humanity's impact on nature. <a href=\"/movie/39995\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"39995\">Long Weekend</a> (1978) took a more sophisticated approach, with nature turning against a bickering couple without explanation."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "What makes Frogs distinctive is its low-key approach to horror. Rather than giant mutants, it's regular-sized frogs, snakes, and lizards shot from menacing angles. Director George McCowan creates tension through creative editing and sound design. The film's environmental message may be heavy-handed, but its execution influenced later nature-revenge films like <a href=\"/movie/31915\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"31915\">Prophecy</a> (1979) and <a href=\"/movie/24831\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"24831\">Piranha</a> (1978). SUBHEAD: Legacy and Influence"
      },
      {
